    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d881">

                <pb facs="0796" n="784" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg784"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M) Turkey/205: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-12-22T19:00:00+02:00">December 22,
                            1942—7 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 23—7:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1338. Department’s 673, December 21, 1 p.m. Conditions given in topic number 3 of
                    Turkish reply as reported in my 1240 (stated in my 1278, December 13 to have
                    been amended as result of final discussions) represent formal reply of Turkish
                    Government in writing. Paragraph 3 of my 1271 represents oral statements made to
                    me by Foreign Minister.</p>
                <pb facs="0797" n="785" xml:id="pg785"/>
                <p>Any difference Department may find between text of formal reply and statements
                    made to me by Foreign Minister is readily explained by statement of British
                    Chargé to Kelley that Numan had indicated “that there was a considerable
                    difference between what Turkey could do in writing to meet British viewpoint and
                    what she could do in practice” (see my 1215, November 28) and British Chargés
                    statement to me that written reply “went as far to meet British desiderata as
                    [the Turks could] be persuaded to put into writing” (see my 1200 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">1280</hi>], December 13).</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
